{"id":"s1-cfo-sceptic-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Never take the AI's 'I'm sure' as evidence, because it sounds just as confident when it has no source. You test checkers on your own data and keep the one whose scoreboard you can defend, and I'd want the cost per month and what it doesn't catch written down.","claims":["When the AI says it is certain, treat that as no proof at all, since it sounds equally sure when it has nothing to back it up. Before relying on any automated checker, run each candidate on your own real examples and keep the one whose results you can justify. Write down what it costs per month and which problems it misses.","When the AI says it is certain, treat that as no evidence at all, since it sounds equally sure when it has nothing to back it up. Before relying on any tool that checks the AI's work, run several candidates on your own real data, pick the one whose results you can justify to others, and write down what it costs each month and which errors it misses.","When the AI tells you it is certain, treat that as no evidence at all, since it sounds equally sure when it has nothing to back it up. Before relying on any tool that checks the AI's work, run it on your own real examples and pick the one whose results you can defend. Write down what it costs per month and which kinds of errors it misses.","Don't accept the AI's statement that it is certain as proof, since it sounds equally sure when it has nothing behind it. Try each candidate verification tool on your own data, pick the one whose results you can justify, and write down its monthly cost and the kinds of errors it misses."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","none","none","security","security"],"why":["The claim says confidence tells you nothing about safety and that you need to run and vet a check. It doesn't say to keep the agent connected to ground truth, which is what A teaches.","Stated certainty tells you nothing about whether the output is safe or correct, so you run an actual check and choose it with care. The claim never says to connect the agent to a source of truth (A) or to hold a judge fixed to measure changes (H).","Stated certainty tells you nothing about the output, so you need to run a check and pick one you've tested on your own examples. That is the 'you can't tell from the output, run the check' idea.","The claim says AI confidence isn't evidence and tells you to run a check on real examples instead of trusting certainty. That is H: you can't judge safety from output, so you run the check.","The first sentence echoes F's 'confidently wrong' point, but the action is to compare candidate checkers on your own examples and keep the best. That is not grounding (F), not running a safety check (H), and not a fixed-judge eval (E).","The claim's action is to bake off candidate checkers on your own examples and record their cost and misses. That is tool selection. It is not grounding (C), not running a check on the output (G), and not a fixed-judge eval loop (A). The only overlap is that confidence is not proof.","Confident-sounding output isn't proof of safety/correctness; run an independent check rather than trusting certainty, and note what it misses.","Confident-sounding output isn't proof; the colleague would act by running a check on their own data and recording what it misses, which is G's practice of running the check."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-cfo-sceptic-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Check AI output against the source, not by asking the AI if it's sure, and pick your checks by testing them on your own data and measuring which one actually catches the errors.","claims":["When an AI gives you an answer, verify it by comparing it to the original source material, not by asking the AI whether it's confident. And before you settle on a way of verifying, try several candidate methods on your own real examples and count how many mistakes each one finds, then keep the one that finds the most.","When an AI gives you an answer, verify it by comparing it to the original documents or data it came from; asking it 'are you sure?' doesn't count. Before relying on any verification method, try a few candidate methods on a sample of your own real data, count how many of the known mistakes each one catches, and use the one that catches the most.","When an AI gives you an answer, verify it by going back to the original material it was based on, never by asking the AI whether it is confident. Before you pick a way to verify, try each candidate on a sample of your own real data where you already know the right answers, count how many of the mistakes each one catches, and keep the one that catches the most.","When an AI gives you an answer, verify it by comparing against the original material it was supposed to draw from; don't just ask it 'are you sure?'. And before choosing how to verify, try several candidate checks on a sample of your own real data, count which one actually flags the mistakes, and use that one."],"picks":["none","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim's core is to test several verification methods on your own data and keep the one that catches the most known errors. That is a different idea from grounding (A), which is about staying connected to the truth and making the gap visible. It is also different from C, which is about checking safety, and from H, which is about a fixed judge for measuring changes.","The claim's core is to check answers against the original documents or data, not the model's own say-so. That is staying connected to the ground. Picking the best check by catch rate is a secondary refinement.","The core is to check the answer against the original source rather than trusting the AI's confidence, which is grounding. Picking the verification method by counting the mistakes each one finds is a secondary detail, not the idea itself.","Checking against the original source instead of the AI's self-reported confidence is the grounding idea; the 'try several methods and keep the best' part is only a way to pick the check.","The claim's core is to verify against the original source material rather than the AI's own confidence, which is grounding. The sample-testing part only picks among grounding checks.","The claim's core is to check answers against the original source rather than the AI's own confidence. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, since a model that can't reach it fills the gap with confident wrong answers.","The core is checking the answer against the original source material the AI should have drawn from, which is grounding. Trying several checks and keeping the best one is a secondary step.","The core action is to check answers against the original source material instead of asking the model 'are you sure?'. That is grounding, staying connected to the truth. Trying several checks on a sample is a supporting method."],"score":0.875,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-cfo-sceptic-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust the AI's own say-so: check its output against the source, and pick the checker by testing it on your own data. Errors compound across steps, so a long workflow with no check is mostly wrong. I never got a cost or payback figure, though.","claims":["When an AI agent hands you results, don't accept its word that they're right: compare them against the original source material. Before relying on any checking method, try it on your own real data to see whether it catches mistakes. For multi-step automated work, put a check between steps, since small errors pile up from step to step.","Whenever an AI produces work, compare it against the original source material rather than accepting its own statement that it's right. Before relying on any checker, try it on a sample of your own real data to see if it catches known errors. Put a check at every step of a multi-step workflow, because mistakes pile up from step to step.","When an AI gives you results, don't accept its statement that they're right; compare them against the original material. Before relying on any automatic checker, try it on a sample of your own real data to see if it catches mistakes. Don't build long multi-step workflows without a verification step, because small mistakes accumulate and the final result will mostly be wrong.","When an AI produces work, don't accept it because it says it's right: compare what it produced against the original material. Before relying on any checking method, try it on a sample of your own data to see whether it catches real mistakes. Also add a check between steps in a multi-step process, because small errors pile up and an unchecked long chain will mostly end up wrong."],"picks":["security","security","output-quality","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim is about verifying AI output with a check you've tested on known errors, not trusting its self-report; that matches 'run the check', not grounding or evals.","The claim is about verifying work against source, testing the checker on known errors, and running checks at every step, which is the practice of running the check rather than trusting output (M4). It is not grounding (F), because the focus is on checking and not on staying connected to truth.","The claim is about staying tied to the source: verify output against the original material instead of trusting the AI's own confidence, and check between steps so errors don't compound. That is grounding. It isn't about safety (E) or eval design (F).","The claim is about verifying AI output by running checks (compare to source, test the checker on your own data, check between steps), which is A's 'run the check' idea, not grounding or evals.","Verify output against source and test the checker on your own data: running the check rather than trusting the agent's say-so.","Don't trust output or the checker's self-report; test the check on real data and verify each step, i.e. run the check because you can't tell safety/correctness from output alone.","Verify agent output against source, test the check on real data, and put checks between steps all mean 'run the check rather than trust output', which is idea B.","Verify agent output against source, test checks on real data, and put checks between steps all say run a check rather than trust output (B). Grounding (F) is about the agent staying connected to truth, not the student's verification practice."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-cfo-sceptic-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Asking the AI 'are you sure?' proves nothing; a real check has to go back to the source, and you should test any check on your own output with a scoreboard before trusting it. Useful logic, but it never gave me a cost or payback.","claims":["When you want to confirm an AI's work is correct, don't just ask it to double-check itself; make it compare against the original data or source. Before relying on any such verification step, try it on cases where you already know the right answer and track how often it catches the errors.","Don't trust an AI's reassurance when you ask it to confirm its own work; verify output by comparing it against the original source material. Before relying on any verification step you've built, run it on cases where you already know the right answers and measure how often it gets them right.","Don't trust an AI's own 'are you sure?' reply as verification; make it check against the original source data. Before relying on any such check, run it on cases where you already know the right answer and see whether it catches the errors.","When you want to confirm an AI's output is right, don't just ask it to double-check itself; make it compare against the original source material. And before relying on any such checking step, run it on a set of cases with known answers and track how often it catches the errors."],"picks":["security","security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security"],"why":["The claim is that you can't trust the output or the agent's own reassurance, so you need a check, and you test that check on cases with known answers. That matches 'can't tell from output, run the check'. It isn't grounding (H), which is about the gap-filling failure, or an eval for comparing changes (E).","The claim says not to trust the agent's own reassurance, to run an independent check, and to test that check on known cases before relying on it. That is E's 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It isn't C, because nothing in it is about measuring whether a change improved the work.","The core move is to check the AI's work against the original source, not its self-review. That is staying connected to the truth so the gap becomes visible. Testing the check on known cases is a secondary calibration step.","The core is to check the AI's work against the original source instead of its own self-review, which is grounding. Testing the check on known-answer cases is a supporting step.","The central move is grounding: verify against the original source, not the model's self-review. Testing the checker on known cases is a supporting step.","The core move is to check output against the original source material instead of asking the model to re-read itself. That is grounding. Testing the check on known-answer cases is a supporting step, not a separate idea.","The core is checking the agent against the original source data instead of its own 'are you sure?' reply, which is staying connected to the ground truth. Safety (C) and fixed-judge evals (G) are not what the claim is about.","Distrusting the AI's self-check and testing the check on known-answer cases is the idea that you can't judge safety from output, so you must run a check."],"score":0.625,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-cto-builder-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"dont trust 'it looked fine'. build a small benchmark with planted fake claims, run a few different checkers against it, and keep the one that actually catches them as a judge that says what it misses. the one i'd have picked wasn't the one that caught the broken citation.","claims":["Before trusting any automated reviewer of your work, test it: make a small set of test items with deliberately inserted errors, run several candidate reviewers on it, and keep the one that actually finds the inserted errors and reports what it can't detect. Don't pick by gut feel, since the reviewer that seemed best to you may miss the real errors.","Before trusting any automated reviewer, build a small test set where you have deliberately inserted known-false statements, run several candidate reviewers on it, and adopt whichever one actually flags the inserted errors, requiring it to state what it fails to catch. Don't pick by gut feel or because the output looked reasonable, since your favorite may miss things like a bad reference.","Before trusting any automated checker to review work, build a small test set where you've deliberately inserted known errors (like invented references), run several candidate checkers on it, and pick the one that finds the most inserted errors and reports what it couldn't catch. Don't pick by gut feel or by whether the output looked reasonable.","Before trusting any automated reviewer, build a small test set of documents with deliberately inserted false statements, run several candidate reviewers on it, and adopt the one that actually flags the false statements, with the reviewer also reporting what it can't detect. Don't pick by gut feel, because my favorite didn't catch a bad reference."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["The claim is about validating a judge against a fixed test set with known planted errors, instead of trusting how its output looks. That is eval methodology: repeatable measurement with a fixed judge.","Building a test set with known inserted errors and comparing candidate reviewers (judges) to see which one actually catches them is eval-building: measuring output against defined criteria with a validated, fixed judge.","Building a test set with planted errors and comparing candidate reviewers on it is an eval: fixed criteria, repeatable measurement, choose by evidence rather than gut feel.","Building a test set with known inserted errors and picking the reviewer by measured performance is an eval: fixed criteria, repeatable, judged against a standard rather than gut feel.","Building a test set with known inserted errors and comparing candidate checkers (judges) against it to pick one is evaluating the evaluator: an eval with a fixed, measured judge, not a one-off safety check (D).","Build a test set with known errors, compare candidate checkers on it, and choose by measured performance: that is a repeatable, fixed-judge eval. B is about running a check on one agent, not about choosing a checker by measurement.","Testing a reviewer against seeded errors and keeping the one that finds them is eval/judge validation: a fixed, repeatable judge you can trust to measure output.","Testing a reviewer against known inserted errors and keeping the one that reliably catches them is the judge-validation core of the eval idea: a fixed, trusted judge that lets you measure change."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-cto-builder-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust one checker or the agent's own \"looks fine\": run a few detectors on your own output, score them against planted bad claims, and keep the winner, because each one misses something different. Write its known limit into the judge file.","claims":["When you want to know if your work is correct, don't rely on one reviewer or on the agent saying it looks good. Build several different review checks, test each against a set of errors you deliberately inserted, and use the one that catches the most. Then record in the instructions for the reviewing step what that check still fails to catch.","When you check an agent's work, don't rely on a single automated check or on the agent saying its output seems okay. Build several different checkers, test each against deliberately inserted false statements to see which catch them, and use the one that catches the most. Then write down in the judge's instructions what that checker is known to miss.","Don't rely on a single checking method or on the agent saying its work looks fine. Try several different checkers on your output, test each one against fake errors you deliberately inserted, and use the one that catches the most. Since every checker has blind spots, record what the chosen one can't catch in the file that describes the reviewer.","Don't rely on a single verification method or on the agent saying its work looks fine. Run several different checks on your output, test each against deliberately inserted known errors to see which catches the most, and use the best one. Note that check's blind spots in the instructions you give the judge."],"picks":["security","security","none","evaluations","security","none","security","security"],"why":["The claim says not to trust the agent's own 'looks fine' and to run checks that you test with planted errors. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, so run the check'. It says nothing about grounding in outside truth, and nothing about a fixed judge measuring changes.","The claim says not to trust the agent's own 'looks fine' and to verify with real checks, which is A's point that you can't judge safety from output and have to run a check. It never mentions grounding or a fixed eval judge.","The claim is about building several checkers, testing them on planted falsehoods and keeping the best. That is neither grounding (F), nor 'run the check' as a safety practice (H), nor a fixed eval judge (E).","Building checkers, testing them against planted errors, keeping the best, and documenting its blind spots is about measuring and fixing the judge so evals are repeatable. That is the eval idea, not E's 'run a check' idea.","Output alone can't show safety; the claim is to run varied checks, validate them with planted errors, and run the best, i.e. practice running the check.","The claim is about choosing the best checker by testing several against seeded errors. That is not grounding (A), not 'run the check instead of waiting for certainty' (C), and not a fixed judge for tracking changes (D).","The claim says not to trust the agent's 'looks good', to build checks, and to test them against inserted errors. That is C's idea that you can't judge output by looking at it and have to run a check. It says nothing about B's fixed judge, criteria, or measuring whether a change improved the work.","The claim is about not trusting the agent's own 'looks good', and about building and running a verified check to find out whether the work is correct, which is the idea in A. It says nothing about measuring whether a change improved the work against a fixed judge, which is E."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-cto-builder-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't pick a check by gut: plant known errors, race several different checks against each other and score them, because none wins everywhere and each misses something. Then keep the judge file with its stated blind spot, and put the check before the agent acts.","claims":["Before trusting any automated check on an agent's work, seed test material with mistakes you already know about, run several different checks on it side by side, and score how many of the planted mistakes each one catches. Expect that no check is best everywhere. Choose one, write down what it tends to miss, and run it before the agent takes its action, not after.","Before trusting any automated check, seed test material with errors you already know about, run several different checks against it, and compare how many each catches, because no single check catches everything. Pick the one that performs best, write down what it still misses next to it, and run it before the agent takes an action, not after.","Before trusting any automated check on your agent's work, seed test material with mistakes you already know about, run several candidate checks on it, and score how many mistakes each one catches; expect no single check to catch everything. Pick the best, keep its instruction file fixed and write down what it tends to miss, and run it before the agent takes its action, not after.","Before trusting any automated check on agent work, deliberately seed work with mistakes you already know about, run several candidate checks on it, and compare how many each one catches. No single check catches everything, so choose one, write down what it tends to miss, and run it before the agent takes its action, not after."],"picks":["security","security","none","none","security","security","evaluations","security"],"why":["The claim is about picking a check that catches known mistakes, accepting that no check is complete, and running it before the agent acts. That matches H (run the check instead of waiting for certainty). It doesn't match A, which is about measuring whether a change improved output with a fixed judge.","The claim is about picking and running a check on agent work before it acts, because output alone can't show whether it's safe or right. It says nothing about grounding in truth, and nothing about measuring whether a change improved the work.","The claim is about benchmarking several checks on seeded known errors and keeping the best one. That is neither grounding/gap-filling (G), nor 'output can't show safety, so run a check' (B), nor a fixed-judge eval for measuring change (F).","The claim is about choosing among checks by testing them on seeded known errors and logging what the winner misses. That is neither grounding (H), nor running a safety check (F), nor a fixed-judge eval for measuring change (G).","The claim is about picking and running a check, with planted known mistakes, before the agent acts. That is the 'you can't tell it's safe from the output, so run the check' practice. It says nothing about grounding in truth or about a fixed judge measuring quality.","The claim is about picking and running a check on an agent's work, before the agent acts, instead of trusting its output. That is A's idea that you can't see safety in the output, so you run a check. F is about measuring whether a change improved the work with a fixed judge, which is a different idea.","Seeding known mistakes, scoring candidate checks, and keeping the judge's instruction file fixed is the eval idea: measure output against defined criteria, repeatably, with a fixed judge.","Seeding known mistakes to test checks, accepting that none catches everything, and running the check before the action is the 'run the check, since you can't judge safety from output' idea. The claim is about testing a safety check, not about building a fixed-judge eval for output quality (C)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-cto-builder-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust one checker or your own 'it looked fine': run several checks on your own agent's output, score them against each other, and keep a judge that states what it can't catch (wrong sources, strategic calls). For agents that act, check between proposal and apply.","claims":["When you build something that checks your agent's work, don't rely on a single reviewer or on your own impression that it looks fine. Run multiple independent reviews and compare their scores, and write down what the reviewer is blind to (e.g. bad sources, strategic judgment calls) so you know where a human must look. If the agent takes actions, put the check after it proposes a change and before the change is applied.","When you test your own AI agent's work, don't rely on a single reviewer or on your own impression that it seemed fine. Have several independent reviewers grade the same output and compare their scores. Keep one reviewer that is explicitly told its blind spots, such as bad sources or judgment calls about strategy. For agents that take actions, put the review step after the agent proposes a change and before the change is applied.","When you build an agent whose output matters, don't accept a single reviewer's pass or your own impression that it looks right. Have multiple independent reviewers grade the output, compare their scores, and use a final reviewer whose notes list the kinds of mistakes it can't detect (bad source material, judgment calls about direction). If the agent changes things in the real world, put the review step after it proposes the change and before the change is carried out.","When you build something that acts on its own, don't accept its results because you looked and they seemed fine, or because one automated check passed. Run multiple independent checks, compare their scores, and use a reviewer whose written description admits the kinds of errors it is blind to, like bad sources or strategic judgment, so you know where to look yourself. For anything that takes real actions, put the review step after it proposes a change and before the change is applied."],"picks":["security","security","evaluations","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Independent reviews, naming the reviewer's blind spots, and a check placed before changes apply are about running a check because output alone can't show safety (M4).","The claim says not to trust your own impression of the output. It says to run an actual check (independent reviews), know where the check is blind, and gate it before changes are applied. That is D's idea, not G's idea of a fixed, repeatable judge measuring whether a change improved the work.","The claim is about grading agent output with several independent judges and comparing their scores, which is the eval idea. It is not about checking safety.","The claim says not to trust a single reviewer or an impression that the output 'seemed fine'. It says to run independent checks, including one told its blind spots, and to gate changes before they are applied. That is the 'you can't tell safety from output, run the check' idea. It doesn't describe a fixed judge or measuring whether a change improved the work, which is what G is about.","The claim is about not trusting output or your own impression: run independent checks, name what the reviewer can't catch, and gate the change before it executes. That is 'you can't tell if it's safe from the output, run the check', not eval measurement of change-over-change improvement.","The claim says not to trust a single pass or how the output looks. Run independent checks, state the checker's blind spots, and gate the real-world action behind review. That is 'you can't tell safety from output, so run the check'.","The claim says not to trust output that looks fine, to run independent checks, and to put a review gate before real actions. That is the idea that you can't judge safety from the output and have to run the check.","The claim says not to trust output because it looked fine or one check passed, to run independent checks, and to place review before actions are applied. That is the 'run the check' safety idea, not eval-based measurement (G) or grounding (F)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-hr-svp-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Asking an agent 'are you sure?' proves nothing, because it sounds equally sure whether or not it has a source; only a check against the actual source helps, and the check has limits (wrong sources, bad judgment) that stay with people.","claims":["Don't ask an AI agent to confirm its own answer, because it will sound just as confident whether or not it has real evidence. Instead, compare its answer against the original material it should have used. Remember that this comparison can still go wrong if the material is bad or the judging is poor, so a person must stay responsible for those parts.","Don't ask an AI agent to confirm its own answer; it will sound just as confident whether it's right or wrong. Instead, compare what it said against the original material it should have drawn from. Also remember that this comparison can fail if you pick the wrong material or judge it badly, so a person still has to own those decisions.","Don't rely on asking the agent whether it's certain; it will sound just as confident whether or not it has real backing. Instead, compare its output against the real underlying material, and remember that a person still has to judge whether that material is the right one and whether the comparison was sound.","Don't ask the agent 'are you sure?' to verify its work, since it will sound just as confident whether or not it has anything behind it. Instead, compare its output against the real source document or data. Remember that this comparison can still go wrong if the source is bad or the checker misjudges, so a person stays responsible for those parts."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","output-quality"],"why":["Core is don't trust the agent's confident self-confirmation; check against the original source material (grounding), while noting the check itself can fail and needs human responsibility.","Comparing the answer against the source material is grounding: stay connected to the truth, since confident self-confirmation can't expose a gap.","Checking the agent against the original source material is grounding: staying connected to the truth, since a confident agent can't tell you it's wrong.","Checking against original source material keeps the agent connected to the ground truth; confident output without grounding is the gap-filling failure the claim describes.","The claim is about confident-sounding output that may lack backing, and the fix is to check it against the real source material. That is grounding, not safety checking.","Checking output against the real underlying material (the ground) because the agent sounds confident without backing is the grounding idea; not F, which is about safety checks.","Output can't show whether it's safe or right (confidence sounds the same either way), so you run an external check against the real source, while a person stays responsible for the check's own limits. That matches D. B is about grounding the agent, not about checking its output afterward.","The claim is that the agent sounds confident whether or not it has grounding, so check against the real source; that is the grounding idea (E), not F's safety-check idea."],"score":0.875,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-hr-svp-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust an AI answer just because it sounds sure, and asking it 'are you sure?' proves nothing; the check has to go back to the real source, and the checker should say what it can't catch. The agent proposes, someone checks, then it acts, and who owns that checking step is a question I'd take to my leadership team.","claims":["When an AI agent gives you an answer, don't accept it because it sounds confident, and don't treat it asking itself again as a check. Verify against the original source of truth, have the verifier state what kinds of errors it can't detect, and decide who is accountable for the verify step before the agent acts.","When an AI agent gives an answer, don't accept it because it sounds confident or because it says yes when asked to double-check. Verify against the original source of truth, make the verifier state what kinds of errors it cannot detect, and decide explicitly who is responsible for the review step before the agent acts.","When an AI tells you something, don't believe it because it sounds confident, and don't treat asking it to double-check itself as verification. Compare its answer against the original source of truth, and have whoever or whatever does the checking state which kinds of errors it cannot detect. Set up the flow so the AI suggests, a separate checker verifies, and only then does it act, and decide who is responsible for that checking step.","When an AI agent gives an answer, don't accept it because it sounds confident or because it says yes when asked to confirm. Verify it against the original underlying source, and have whatever does the verifying state which kinds of errors it cannot detect. Next week, bring to leadership the question of who is responsible for that verification step before the agent acts."],"picks":["output-quality","security","output-quality","output-quality","security","security","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim says not to trust confident answers, and to verify against the original source of truth and state which errors the check can't see. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth and making the gap visible.","The claim is about running an independent check on agent output (not self-recheck, with named blind spots and an accountable owner) rather than trusting confident output. That is D's 'run the check'. B is about connecting the agent to ground truth, which the claim doesn't say.","The claim is about verifying against the original source of truth rather than trusting confident-sounding answers, which is grounding, not output-safety checks (D) or measurement with a fixed judge (E).","The core move is to distrust confident or agreeable answers and verify against the original source of truth. That matches grounding and making the gap visible, not safety checks on output.","The claim is about building a separate check (suggest, verify, then act, with a named owner and known blind spots), because confident output can't show whether the agent is safe, and that is the idea in E. It is not mainly about keeping the agent connected to ground truth, which is what A is about.","The claim's core is that output and self-checks can't show whether the agent is right or safe, so a separate checker has to run the check before the agent acts. Grounding in a source (B) is only one part of it.","The claim centres on checking the agent's confident answer against the original source, which is grounding. The other parts, the verifier's blind spots and who owns the check, are secondary.","Verify against the original source rather than trusting confident answers is grounding: staying connected to truth so confident-but-wrong gaps become visible."],"score":0.625,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-hr-svp-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"An AI answer sounds just as sure whether or not it has a source, and asking it 'are you sure?' proves nothing, so you have to check against the actual source and know what your check misses. Where the output turns into an action, a person who understands those limits should review it first, and someone has to own that role.","claims":["Don't trust an AI's answer because it sounds confident, and don't treat it asking itself 'are you sure?' as verification. Compare its output against the original source, and know what that comparison can't catch. Where its output triggers a real action, have a person who understands those blind spots review it first, and name who is responsible for doing that.","When an AI gives you an answer, don't trust its confident tone or its own assurance when you ask it to double-check. Compare the answer against the original source yourself, and know which kinds of errors that comparison would not catch. If the output will trigger a real action, have someone who understands those blind spots look it over before it goes out, and name a specific person responsible for doing that.","Don't trust an AI's answer because it sounds confident or because it says yes when you ask if it's sure. Compare it to the real source document or data, and know which kinds of errors that comparison would still miss. Before AI output triggers a real action, have a named person who understands those blind spots approve it, and assign that responsibility to someone specific.","When an AI gives you an answer, don't trust how confident it sounds, and don't treat it re-confirming itself as a check. Compare the answer to the real underlying source, and know which kinds of errors that comparison would not catch. Before the output triggers any real action, have a named person who understands those blind spots review it, and make sure someone is explicitly assigned that job."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Confident output and self-asking 'are you sure?' aren't verification; you need to run an actual check (compare to source, human review), which is B's 'can't tell safety from output, run the check', not G's grounding gap.","The claim says to check output rather than trust it or self-verify, to know the check's blind spots, and to name a human owner before real actions. That matches 'you can't tell from output, run the check', not grounding.","The claim says confidence and self-assurance don't prove correctness, and you need an independent check with a named owner. That is 'you can't tell if the agent is safe from its output; run the check'. It is not A, because the point is not mainly about staying connected to a source.","The claim says tone and self-assurance can't show an answer is safe, so you run an external check and name a person who owns the review before real action. That is idea A. It isn't C, which is about the agent staying connected to the ground truth.","The claim says confident output and self-confirmation prove nothing, so you need an independent check, with a named human reviewer before any action. That is F's 'can't tell it's safe from the output, run the check', not H's idea of staying connected to the source.","The claim says confidence and self-confirmation aren't checks and calls for an independent check plus a named human reviewer before action, which is G's 'you can't tell it's safe from output; run the check.' A would stress comparing to source, but the claim's focus is the check-and-review practice.","The claim says you can't trust confident output or the AI's own 'yes I'm sure'; run an independent check against the source, know its blind spots, and have a named person approve before action. That is F's idea: run a check, because output alone can't show safety. H is the nearest alternative, but the claim is about verifying and approving, not about gaps being filled when the agent can't reach the source.","The claim says output can't be trusted at face value, so you run a check (compare to the source, know its blind spots) and name an approver before action. That is the safety-check idea, not grounding."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-hr-svp-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Asking the AI 'are you sure?' proves nothing. A real check goes back to the source, and every checker has limits, so people decisions stay with humans.","claims":["When the AI says it checked its own work, don't treat that as verification. Verify by comparing the output against the original data or documents it came from. Any automated check has blind spots, so decisions that affect people should be made by a person, not left to a checker.","Don't trust an AI's own confirmation when you ask it to double-check its work. Verify output by going back to the original source material and comparing. Any automated check has blind spots, so a person must make the final call on decisions about people (hiring, performance, etc.).","Don't trust an AI's own confirmation when you ask it to double-check its work. Verify output by going back to the original source material. Any automated verification has blind spots, so a person should make the final call on decisions about people (hiring, evaluation, and so on).","When an AI tells you its work is right, don't take its word for it, and don't count a second prompt like 'are you sure?' as verification. Check the output against the original source or data. Know that any automated check misses some things, so a person makes the final call on decisions that affect people (hiring, evaluations, and the like)."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","output-quality","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim says you can't trust the agent's own report that it checked its work, and that you need an independent check. That is idea C (can't tell from output, run the check). The human-decision part is about safety, not about grounding the agent in source truth.","Output (the AI's self-check) can't establish safety; you need an independent check run against the source, with a person deciding where people are affected, which is idea E, not grounding (C) or evals (A).","Don't trust the AI's self-confirmation; run an independent check against source (and a human for people decisions) — you can't judge safety from output alone, you need a real check.","The claim says AI self-confirmation can't be trusted and you need an independent check against source, plus human sign-off. That matches 'you can't tell it's safe from output alone; run the check'.","Checking output against the original source/data rather than trusting the AI's confident self-assessment is grounding; 'are you sure?' isn't contact with the ground.","Can't trust the agent's self-assessment or output alone; run an independent check against source, with a human making the final call because checks miss things.","Self-confirmation can't be trusted; you need an external check (back to source, human final call) rather than judging by output, which is the safety-check idea. Grounding (A) is about gap-filling, not verification.","Don't trust the AI's self-confirmation; verify independently with a check and keep a human on high-stakes decisions — it's about checking safety/correctness, not output alone."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ingrid-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"The agent makes things up when it has no source and sounds just as sure either way, so you have to run a check that goes back to the sources, and the saved judge file should say what it can't catch (grounded only means traceable, not true).","claims":["When the agent has no source to draw on, it will invent an answer and sound just as confident as when it is right, so after it produces something, run a separate check that compares each statement against the original material. Keep the checker's instructions in a saved file, and write into that file what the check cannot catch: a statement that traces back to a source only proves it came from there, not that it is correct.","When the agent has no source for something, it will invent an answer and sound just as confident as when it's right, so confidence tells you nothing. Add a verification step that checks each claim against the original sources. When you save the checker's instructions for reuse, write down what it cannot detect: a claim passing means it can be traced to a source, not that it is true.","When an AI system has no source to draw on, it will invent an answer and sound as confident as when it is right. So build a step that traces each of its statements back to the original documents, and keep a written note beside that step listing what it cannot catch. Being traceable to a source only shows the statement matches the source; it does not show the statement is true.","When the agent has no source to draw on, it will invent an answer and sound as confident as when it has one, so you can't judge by tone. Run a separate check that traces each claim back to the original material. Keep the checker's instructions saved in a file, and write into it what that check cannot catch: a claim that traces to a source only shows where it came from, not that it is correct."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim centers on the agent inventing confident answers when it lacks a source, and on checking statements against the original material. That is grounding: stay connected to the truth and make the gap visible.","The claim says the agent invents answers with the same confidence when it has no source, and that you check each statement against the source material. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth and making the gap visible.","Agent invents confident answers when it lacks a source; the fix is verifying claims against sources (staying connected to ground truth), and noting the checker only confirms traceability, not truth. This is grounding.","Agent invents answers without a source and sounds confident; the fix is verifying claims against original sources (staying connected to the ground), with the caveat that passing means traceable, not true.","Core is that without a source the AI fills the gap confidently and wrongly, and the response is to stay connected to ground and make the gap visible, including noting what the check can't catch.","No source → confident invention, and the fix is a traceability step that makes the gap visible (with its limits noted); this is grounding, staying connected to truth.","No source means the agent fills the gap confidently, so tone can't tell you; the remedy is tracing claims back to original material, i.e. staying connected to the truth (grounding).","Agent invents confident answers when it has no source, so you trace claims back to the material (grounding) and note the check can't verify correctness."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ingrid-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't ask the agent 'are you sure'. Plant known errors in your own briefing, run several checkers against it, and score which one catches them, because 'grounded' only means traceable to a source and doesn't mean true. The saved judge file also says what it can't catch, which is a wrong source or a tilted framing.","claims":["Don't just ask an AI assistant to confirm its own work. Deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into your briefing, run a handful of different reviewers on it, and see which one finds them. Being tied to a cited source only shows where a claim came from, not that it is correct. Keep the saved reviewer instructions, including its stated blind spots: it won't catch a source that is itself wrong or a slanted way of presenting the facts.","Don't just ask an AI assistant whether it's confident in its work. Instead, deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into a document you give it, have several different reviewers check that document, and see which reviewer finds the mistakes. Remember that 'every statement traces back to a source' is not the same as 'every statement is correct', so also write down in the reviewer's saved instructions what kinds of problems it will miss, such as a source that is itself wrong or a slanted way of presenting the facts.","Don't just ask the agent whether it's sure of its work. Deliberately insert known mistakes into your own briefing, run several different checkers on it, and see which checker finds the planted mistakes. Remember that 'grounded' only means a claim points back to a source, not that the claim is correct. When you save the winning checker's instructions, also write down what it cannot catch, such as a bad source or a slanted framing.","Before trusting any automated checker of your work, deliberately insert known mistakes into a document you prepared, run multiple reviewers on it, and see which ones find them. Asking the agent to confirm itself proves nothing. Also, 'every claim points to a source' is not the same as 'every claim is correct', so write down in the saved reviewer instructions what it can't detect: sources that are themselves wrong, and a slanted presentation."],"picks":["security","security","security","none","security","security","evaluations","none"],"why":["The claim is to stop asking the agent how confident it is and to run a check, by planting known errors and seeing which reviewer catches them. It also says to record what the check misses. That is 'you can't tell from the output, run the check', not grounding or a fixed-judge eval.","Seeding known mistakes to test which reviewer catches them, and documenting what a check can't catch (traceable ≠ correct), is about verifying safety/quality by running a check rather than trusting self-reported confidence.","Don't trust the agent's 'I'm sure'; run a check, and test the checker with planted mistakes, noting its blind spots.","The claim is about testing checkers by planting known errors and recording their blind spots. That is not the grounding idea (A), which is about staying connected to the truth source. It is also not the safety-check idea (B) or the fixed-judge eval idea (E).","The claim is about validating a checker by planting known mistakes and not trusting the agent's self-confirmation. It also says to record what the check can't detect, which is the 'run the check, don't assume it's safe' practice.","The claim says output or self-confirmation can't show an agent's work is sound. You have to run an independent check, test it with planted errors, and state what it can't catch. That is D's idea. It doesn't carry E's idea of staying connected to the truth, or G's idea of measuring whether a change improved the output.","The claim is about building a fixed, saved reviewer (judge) and testing it on seeded known errors, which is a repeatable eval. It is not about safety checks or grounding.","The claim is about testing reviewers by planting known errors and picking the one that catches them, with citations shown as provenance only. That fits none of the eight ideas: it isn't grounding's 'stay connected to truth', the safety-check idea, or eval-with-a-fixed-judge."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-ingrid-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"An agent's invented content sounds exactly like its grounded content, so asking it \"are you sure?\" is no check: you have to test against the sources, on your own output, and keep a judge file that records what it catches and what it misses.","claims":["When an AI agent gives you facts or content, don't ask it to confirm itself, because its made-up material reads just as confident as its sourced material. Instead, check its output against the original sources yourself, and keep a running file of what your checker has caught and missed.","When an AI agent gives you content, don't ask it to confirm itself, because made-up material reads just as confidently as material drawn from real sources. Instead, compare its output against the actual source documents yourself, and keep a running file that notes which errors your checker has caught and which it has let through.","Don't rely on asking the AI whether it's certain, because made-up material reads just as confident as properly sourced material. Next week, check its work against the original sources myself, run that check on my own outputs, and keep a running file that notes which errors my check caught and which it let through.","When an AI agent's output matters, don't ask the agent whether it's sure, because its made-up material reads just as confident as its sourced material. Instead, check its claims against the original sources yourself, on your own work, and keep a running file noting which errors your checker caught and which it let through."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["Confident made-up content, verifying against original sources (staying connected to ground truth) is the grounding idea; D is about running checks on safety, not source truth.","The claim is about made-up material reading as confident as sourced material, and the fix is to check against original sources. That is grounding.","The claim is about confident-but-fabricated output and the fix is to check it against the real source documents. That is grounding: stay connected to the truth, and make the gap visible.","The claim is about confident fabrication where the agent can't reach the source, and about checking output against the real documents. That is grounding. The catch/miss log is a side detail.","The claim is about made-up material reading as confident as sourced material, and the action is to check against original sources. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth and making the gap visible.","The claim is about made-up material sounding as confident as sourced material, and about checking against the original sources. That is grounding and the confidently-wrong gap, not a general safety check.","Made-up claims read as confident as sourced ones, so you check them against the original sources. That is grounding: the gap between agent and truth has to be made visible.","The claim's core is that made-up material reads as confident as sourced material, so you check claims against the original sources. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, with the gap made visible."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ingrid-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Asking the agent 'are you sure?' checks nothing. A check has to go back to the source files, so run a few candidate checks on your own material, score them, and keep the best one as a saved judge file. It only catches invented content, though; a wrong source or a wrong call still needs you.","claims":["When you want to know if an AI agent's work is right, don't just ask it to confirm itself. Write a check that sends it back to your original files to compare. Try several versions of that check on your own material, score how well each one catches mistakes, and save the best as a reusable file. Remember it only catches made-up content. If the source files are wrong, or the decision itself is bad, you still have to catch that yourself.","When you want to know whether the agent's work is right, don't just ask it to confirm itself. Write a check that sends it back to the original source material and compares the output against that. Try several versions of the check on your own data, score how well each one catches errors, and save the best as a reusable file to run every time. Remember it only catches made-up content; if the source is wrong or the decision itself is bad, you still have to catch that yourself.","Don't ask the model whether it is sure of its work. Build a check that compares the output against the original source documents. Try several versions of that check on your own material, score how well each catches problems, and store the best one as a reusable file. Expect it to catch only fabricated content; you must still verify the sources and the judgment calls yourself.","When I want to know whether an AI's work is trustworthy, I won't just ask it to confirm itself. I'll build a saved checker that compares the output against the original source files, try several versions on my own material, score each, and keep the best. I'll remember it only catches made-up content, so I still have to verify the sources are right and make the judgment calls myself."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","evaluations"],"why":["The claim is about checking agent output against the original source files and catching made-up content. That is grounding, and it names the limit that grounding can't fix bad sources.","The claim is about checking the agent's work against the original source files, because ungrounded content is made up. It also says the check can't catch bad sources or bad decisions. That is grounding, not a repeatable eval of changes (D) or a safety check (F).","The checker compares output against the original source files and catches made-up content, and the colleague still verifies the sources themselves. That is grounding. 'Try versions, score, keep the best' is only the method, not a fixed-judge eval idea.","The checker compares output against the original source files to catch made-up content, and the colleague still verifies the sources themselves. That is grounding: stay connected to the truth and make the gap visible.","The claim is to check the agent's output against the original source material and to catch made-up content. That is the grounding idea of staying connected to the truth. It is not an eval with a fixed judge that tracks whether changes improve the work.","The claim is about checking agent output against the original source material, which is grounding, and it notes the check only catches fabricated content. It is not B (a general safety check) or H (a fixed judge that measures changes).","The claim centres on checking output against source documents to catch fabricated content, and on verifying the sources yourself. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth so the gap where the agent invents things becomes visible.","Build a fixed check, score versions of it repeatably, keep the best as a reusable file: that is eval practice (a judge held fixed, measure whether changes help), not grounding or safety."],"score":0.875,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ops-director-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"AI sounds just as sure when it makes things up, so you have to check its work against the real source, not just ask it if it is sure.","claims":["When the AI gives you facts, quotes, numbers or sources, open the original document or data yourself and compare. Don't rely on asking the AI whether it is confident, because it will sound equally certain whether it is right or invented the answer.","When the AI gives me a fact, a number, or a quote I plan to use, I look it up in the original document or official source myself. Asking the AI 'are you sure?' proves nothing, because it will sound confident whether it is right or wrong.","When the AI gives you facts, figures or citations, open the original document or data and compare them yourself; asking the AI whether it is confident proves nothing, since it will sound equally certain whether it is right or invented the answer.","When the AI gives you facts, names, numbers or sources, open the original document or data yourself and compare. Asking the AI whether it is confident tells you nothing, because it sounds equally certain when it is inventing."],"picks":["output-quality","security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["Verifying facts against the original source because the AI sounds confident either way is grounding: staying connected to truth when the model fills gaps confidently and wrongly.","The colleague won't trust the AI's confident output or its 'are you sure?' reply. They run an independent check against the original source, which is idea F: you can't judge an output by looking at it, so run the check.","Verifying facts against the original source instead of trusting the AI's confidence is grounding: staying connected to the truth because the AI fills gaps confidently and wrongly.","Verify against the original source because the AI fills gaps confidently and plausibly wrong; this is grounding, not a safety check (D).","Verifying AI's facts and citations against the original source because it fills gaps confidently and plausibly wrong is grounding (B).","Verifying against the original source instead of trusting the AI's confident tone is grounding: staying connected to the truth because the AI fills gaps confidently and plausibly wrong.","The claim says to check facts against the original source, because the AI sounds equally confident when it invents. That is the grounding idea: stay connected to the truth, since the AI fills gaps confidently and wrongly.","Checking against the original source because the AI sounds equally confident when inventing is the grounding idea: stay connected to the truth, since the AI fills gaps confidently and wrongly."],"score":0.875,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ops-director-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"AI sounds sure even when it made it up, so you can't just ask it 'are you sure'. Something has to check against the real source, and a person checks before it sends or changes anything.","claims":["Don't trust an AI's own confidence or its reassurance when you ask if it's right, because it can sound certain about things it invented. Set up a check that compares its output to the actual source of truth, and have a human review and approve before anything gets sent out or changed.","Don't trust an AI's own confidence or its answer to 'are you sure?'. Set up a separate check that compares its output to the real source, and have a person review before anything is sent out or changed.","Don't trust an AI's own confidence or its answer to 'are you sure'. Set up a separate check that compares its output with the actual source data, and have a human approve before anything gets sent out or changed.","Don't trust the AI's own confidence or ask it to double-check itself, because it can state invented things as fact. Have a separate check compare its output to the actual source, and have a person review before anything is sent out or changed."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim is about the agent inventing things with confidence and the fix of checking against the actual source of truth. That is grounding, not a general safety check.","The claim is about the agent inventing things with confidence, and the fix is to compare its output against the actual source of truth. That is grounding. B is about verifying that an agent is safe, which the claim doesn't address.","The claim is about the AI stating invented things as fact and checking its output against the actual source. That is grounding: stay connected to the truth, because the gap gets filled confidently and wrongly.","The claim is about invented facts stated confidently, and the fix is to check the output against the actual source. That is grounding. H is about safety checks, not fabrication.","Distrusting the agent's own confidence and running an independent check plus human approval matches 'can't tell safe from output, run the check'; E is about grounding/hallucination, but the claim's focus is verification/safety gating.","Don't trust the AI's own confidence or 'are you sure'; run a separate check and verify rather than judging safety from output.","Can't judge safety from the agent's output or its self-assurance; run a separate check against the real source plus human review, which is G's 'you need a way to check, run the check'.","Can't judge safety from the agent's own output or self-reported confidence; run a separate check against the real source (and human review) — the practice is running the check."],"score":0.5,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ops-director-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Check the agent's work against the real source file, not by asking it 'are you sure', because small mistakes add up over many steps. Different checkers catch different mistakes, so you test a few and keep the best one.","claims":["When the agent finishes a task, don't ask it whether it's confident. Compare its output against the actual original file or data yourself, because tiny errors pile up over many steps. Try several different kinds of checks on the same work, see which one catches the most mistakes, and keep using that one.","When the agent finishes a piece of work, compare it against the original source material yourself instead of asking the agent to confirm it's right. Small errors compound over many steps. Try several different kinds of checks, see which one catches the most mistakes, and keep using that one.","When you have an agent do multi-step work, don't ask it to confirm its own output. Compare what it produced against the original source material yourself, because tiny errors pile up across steps. Try several different kinds of verification, see which one catches the most problems, and keep using that one.","When an agent has done work, verify it by comparing against the original source material yourself, rather than asking the agent whether it is confident. Small errors accumulate across many steps. Try several different verification methods, see which one catches the most mistakes, and use that one."],"picks":["output-quality","security","security","none","output-quality","security","output-quality","security"],"why":["Verifying output against the original source file/data yourself, rather than trusting the agent's self-reported confidence, is staying connected to the ground truth.","Don't trust the agent's self-reported confidence; run an independent check against the original data. Comparing several checks to keep the best one is a side detail, not the core idea.","The claim says to run an independent check on the output, not trust the agent's self-confirmation, and to try different checks and keep the one that works. That is H's idea that you need a way to check, and that running the check is the practice.","The claim is about checking output against the source yourself and picking the best verification method. It doesn't carry A's point that output can't show safety, and it doesn't carry H's point that an agent fills gaps confidently and wrongly when it can't reach the ground.","The claim's core is to check the agent's work against the original source instead of asking the agent to confirm it. That is staying connected to the ground truth, which is grounding.","Checking the work yourself against the source rather than trusting the agent's confirmation, and running checks, matches 'can't tell safety from output, run the check'.","The core act is checking the agent's work against the original source material rather than trusting its confidence, which is staying connected to the ground truth.","The claim says to run an independent check against the source instead of trusting the agent's confidence, which is G's 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It never says the agent fills gaps with plausible-sounding errors, which is the core of B."],"score":0.375,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ops-director-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"AI sounds just as sure when it is wrong, so don't ask it 'are you sure' - check the claim against the real source. And let the AI suggest, something check, then act, like for dispatch messages.","claims":["When an AI gives an answer, don't try to confirm it by asking the AI whether it's certain, since it will sound confident either way. Look the claim up in the original source yourself. Also, set up the AI so it only proposes what to do, a person (or a separate check) verifies it, and only then does the action happen, for example before it sends any messages out.","When the AI gives you a factual answer, don't ask it to confirm itself, because it will sound equally confident whether right or wrong; instead look the fact up in the original source yourself. Also, for actions with consequences, such as sending messages, have the AI propose the action, verify it, and only then carry it out.","When the AI gives you a factual claim, look it up in the original source yourself instead of asking the AI to confirm itself, because it sounds equally confident whether right or wrong. For actions with consequences, such as sending messages, have the AI propose them, verify them, and only then let it carry them out.","When the AI gives you a factual answer, don't ask it to confirm itself, because it will sound equally confident whether right or wrong. Go look at the original source yourself and compare. When the AI is going to take an action, such as sending messages, have it propose the action first, verify it, and only then let it carry it out."],"picks":["security","security","output-quality","output-quality","security","security","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim says you can't judge output by its confidence, so run an independent check: look up the source, and verify a proposed action before it runs. That is D's idea of running the check, not H's idea of keeping the agent grounded.","Both halves say you can't judge from the output or the AI's confidence, so you run an independent check (look up the source, verify before acting). F is about the agent staying connected to ground truth, not about you checking its output.","Verify claims against the original source because the AI sounds equally confident when wrong is grounding (staying connected to truth, confident-but-wrong gap-filling).","Verify against the original source because the AI fills gaps confidently and plausibly wrong; grounding to truth, not self-confirmation.","The claim says not to trust the AI's confident output, and to run an independent check (look up the source, or gate actions behind human or separate verification) before acting. That is 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It never mentions the agent filling gaps when cut off from the truth, which is what D is about.","The claim says not to trust the AI's confident output or its self-report. It says to run an external check (look up the source, or have a person or separate check verify) before acting, and that is G's 'run the check' idea. It doesn't say the AI fills gaps when it can't reach the truth, which is what B is about.","Don't trust the AI's self-confirmation; check against the original source (ground truth) because it sounds equally confident when wrong — the grounding idea. The action-proposal part is secondary.","Don't trust the AI's self-confirmation (confident whether right or wrong); check against the original source to stay connected to ground truth. That is grounding."],"score":0.5,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-veteran-director-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"An agent's confident tone tells you nothing about whether it's right, so you check against the actual sources and test the checker on your own data instead of trusting it or asking it 'are you sure'.","claims":["Don't believe an AI's answer because it sounds sure of itself. Verify its output against the original source material, and before you rely on any automated checker, run it on your own real data to see whether it catches real mistakes. Asking the AI to double-check itself doesn't count as verification.","When an AI agent reports a result, don't take its sureness as a sign it's correct. Compare what it says against the original source material, and before relying on any automated checker, run it on examples from your own data where you already know the right answer. Don't just ask the agent to double-check itself.","Don't judge an agent's output by how sure it sounds. Compare what it says against the original source material, and before you rely on any automated checker, run it on examples from your own data where you already know the right answer. Asking the agent to re-confirm itself doesn't count as a check.","Never accept an agent's output because it sounds sure of itself. Check its claims against the original source material, and before you rely on any automated checker, run it on your own real data where you already know the right answers to see whether it catches the errors. Don't just ask the agent to reconsider its answer."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The core is grounding: confident output isn't evidence, so verify claims against the original source rather than asking the agent to reconsider. The checker-validation step supports that.","The claim tells you to distrust confident output and check claims against the original source. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, because a confident agent fills gaps with plausible wrong answers.","The claim centers on not trusting confident-sounding output and checking it against the original source, which is grounding: staying connected to the truth rather than letting the agent fill gaps.","The claim says you can't judge output by how sure it sounds, that you need a real check (validated against known answers, not self-confirmation), and that you should run it. That matches D's 'can't tell from the output, run the check'. H is about keeping the agent connected to the source so it doesn't fill gaps.","Core is running a check on real data to catch real mistakes rather than trusting confident-sounding output or self-review; that matches 'can't tell safety from output, run the check'.","Verifying output against the source and testing a checker on real data means running the check rather than trusting how the output looks; H covers this, not E's grounding of the agent.","The claim says not to trust the agent's confidence, to run an independent check, and to test that checker on cases with known answers. That is idea B (you can't judge safety from output, so run the check). It never mentions giving the agent access to ground truth, which is what idea D is about.","Confidence isn't evidence of correctness; run a check against known-answer examples rather than trusting the agent or its self-review, which is the 'you can't tell from output, run the check' idea."],"score":0.375,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-veteran-director-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"An agent that has no source will make things up and sound just as sure, so asking it 'are you sure?' proves nothing; you have to check against the source, and you can test your checkers on your own material and score them. I still don't know what it does for my business.","claims":["Before trusting an AI agent's factual output, give it the real source material and check its answer against that source yourself; asking the agent whether it is sure tells you nothing, since it sounds equally confident when wrong. You can also try your checking method on your own documents where you already know the right answers, and count how often it catches mistakes.","When an AI tool gives you facts or recommendations, don't ask it to confirm itself. Compare its output against the original documents or data it should be based on. Without that reference material it will invent details and sound confident. You can also test your review method by running it on material where you already know the right answers and counting how often it catches the errors.","When an AI tool gives you facts, don't ask it whether it's sure. Compare what it says against the real original document or data, and build a small test with your own known material to see which of your checking methods actually catches its errors.","When an AI gives you facts, don't ask it whether it's sure; check what it says against the original documents or data. Before you rely on any checking method, try it on material where you already know the right answers and score how often it catches mistakes."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["Giving the agent real source material and checking against it is grounding; asking 'are you sure' fails because it sounds confident when wrong.","The claim is about staying connected to real source material and checking against it because the agent sounds confident when wrong, which is the grounding idea.","Check output against the real source (the ground) instead of asking the model if it's sure — grounding, staying connected to truth.","Checking against the real original source/data is grounding: staying connected to truth rather than asking the model if it's sure.","The claim is about facts: check them against the original documents or data, not the model's own confidence. That is grounding, staying connected to the truth. The known-answers test is a supporting step, not the main idea.","The claim says to check an AI's facts against the original documents or data instead of trusting its confidence. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, because the AI fills gaps confidently and wrongly.","Compare against source documents because without grounding the AI invents confident details; this is the grounding idea (F), not the general eval or safety-check ideas.","Compare output against original source documents because without ground it fills gaps confidently and wrongly is the grounding idea; the eval-testing bit is secondary."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-veteran-director-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Asking the AI 'are you sure?' proves nothing; a check has to go back to the actual source, and you can test any checker by planting known errors and counting what it catches, since no single one catches everything.","claims":["When you want to know if an AI's output is right, don't ask the AI to confirm itself; make the check compare the output against the original material. And before you trust any checker, test it by seeding in mistakes you already know about and seeing how many it finds. Expect it to miss some, so don't rely on just one checker.","When you want an AI's work verified, don't ask it to double-check itself. Make the check compare the output against the original source material. And before trusting any checker, seed some deliberate mistakes into test material and count how many it finds. Expect it to miss some, so don't rely on just one checker.","When you want to know if an AI's output is right, don't ask the AI to confirm it; make the check go look at the original source material. And before trusting any checking step, hide some deliberate mistakes in a test set and count how many it finds, because no checker finds everything.","When you want to know if an AI's output is right, don't ask it to confirm itself. Have the check go back to the original source material. And before trusting any checker, slip in mistakes you already know about and see how many it finds. Expect it to miss some, so don't rely on just one checker."],"picks":["security","security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim is about running an independent check on output, validating the checker with seeded known flaws, and accepting that it misses some. That is the \"run the check, don't wait for certainty\" idea, not grounding or eval measurement.","The claim is about verifying output by running an independent check, testing the checker with seeded known errors, and not relying on one checker. That matches 'you can't tell from the output; run the check'. F is the nearest alternative, but the claim is about checking, not about staying connected to ground truth.","The claim's core is to check against the original source instead of asking the AI to confirm. That is staying connected to the ground truth. The seeded-mistakes test serves that discipline by making the checker's gaps visible.","The core move is to check the output against the original source material rather than ask the AI to confirm itself, which is grounding. The seeded-mistakes step only tests how well that checker catches errors.","The core move is to check output against the original source material, which is grounding. Seeding mistakes and using several checkers only support that.","Verifying by comparing to source, seeding known errors to test the checker, and not relying on one checker is the 'run the check, don't trust the output' safety idea; it's not about fixed judges measuring change (D) or grounding gaps (C).","The claim's core action is to check output against the original source material (grounding), not to have the agent confirm itself. Seeding known errors and using several checkers are supporting tactics for that.","Checking against original source material (staying connected to ground truth) rather than self-confirmation is grounding; planted-error testing and multiple checkers are supporting practice, not the eval-definition idea."],"score":0.625,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-veteran-director-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't ask the AI 'are you sure?', it just repeats itself confidently; test your checkers on your own material with planted errors and score them. I'd still want real numbers, not illustrative ones.","claims":["Never rely on asking the AI to double-check its own work, because it will just restate its answer with the same confidence. Instead, build a checker and test it: take your own real material, insert errors you put there deliberately, and measure how many of them the checker catches. Report actual measured figures from real runs, not made-up example numbers.","Don't verify an AI's work by asking it to confirm itself, because it will just restate its answer with confidence. Instead, build a check by taking your own real material, deliberately inserting known mistakes, running the checker on it, and scoring how many it catches. Use actual measured results from your own data to judge the checker, not made-up example numbers.","Don't verify an AI's work by asking it whether it's sure; it will just restate its answer with confidence. Instead, build a checker and test it on your own documents after deliberately inserting known mistakes, then score how many it catches. Use real measured results rather than made-up examples when judging it.","Don't rely on asking the AI to confirm its own work; it will just restate its answer with confidence. Instead, build your own checks and test them on your own material: plant known mistakes in it, run the checks, and measure how many of the planted mistakes they catch. Use real measured results, not made-up example numbers."],"picks":["security","security","evaluations","evaluations","security","security","evaluations","security"],"why":["The claim says self-review can't be trusted, so build a checker and prove it catches deliberately planted errors with measured results. That is 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. It says nothing about criteria, a fixed judge, or comparing changes, which is what F is about.","It says self-review can't be trusted, so build a checker and measure its catch rate on planted errors; that is G's 'run the check' idea, not B's fixed-judge evals.","Building a repeatable measurement with a fixed judge: seed known errors in your own material, score catch rate, judge the checker by measured results.","Seeding known errors into your own material and scoring the checker's catch rate describes building a fixed, repeatable eval/judge measured against criteria; G is about running a check, but the claim's core is measuring the checker.","Core is that you can't trust the output or the AI's self-confirmation, so you build and run checks, here validated by planted mistakes.","The claim says not to trust the agent's own confident output, to build and run a check, and to test the check on planted mistakes. That is 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check', not the eval idea of comparing versions with a fixed judge.","Building a checker, seeding known errors, and scoring catch rate on real data is eval practice: measuring output against defined criteria with a fixed, repeatable judge.","The claim says you can't verify by asking the AI if it's sure, so you build a checker and run it, scoring it against planted errors. That is 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It never mentions comparing versions with a fixed judge, which is what C is about."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
